  "market_structure": {
    "data_inputs": [
      "high",
      "low"
    ],
    "outputs": [
      "bias",
      "last_high_price",
      "last_high_bar",
      "last_low_price",
      "last_low_bar",
      "has_flip",
      "flip_bearish",
      "flip_price",
      "flip_bar",
      "flip_strength",
      "swing_depth",
      "bar_index"
    ],
    "warmup_bars": 0,
    "required_params": [
      "swing_strength"
    ],
    "_note": "These 12 flat names are correct for the .ta()/LazyFrame batch API -- verified against the polars_expr struct built in quantwave-py/src/plugins/custom_6.rs::market_structure_output (12 named Struct fields: bias, last_high_price, last_high_bar, last_low_price, last_low_bar, has_flip, flip_bearish, flip_price, flip_bar, flip_strength, swing_depth, bar_index) and confirmed by tests/python/test_abstract_api.py::test_output_names_arity_matches_actual_ta_output (expects arity 12, not 6). This is a SEPARATE code path from the streaming class (quantwave.streaming_class('market_structure')): its .next() returns MarketStructureStateResult (quantwave-py/src/indicators.rs), which has only 6 top-level attrs (bias, last_swing_high, last_swing_low, current_flip, swing_depth_used, bar_index) -- last_swing_high/last_swing_low are Option<SwingPointResult> (bar/price/is_high) and current_flip is Option<FlipEventResult> (is_bearish/price/bar/structure_strength), all nested sub-objects with no flat equivalent. Batch (.ta()) and streaming (streaming_class()) market_structure therefore expose genuinely different output shapes -- a real API-parity gap, not a metadata bug, and out of scope for this fix; only the .ta() shape belongs in this `outputs` field since that's what `_batch_capable()`/output-arity tests check."
  },
